      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fairfield grew around Schencks Station, a rail stop named for landowner Aaron Schenck, near present Symmes Road and State Route 4.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairfield%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Elisha Morgan Mansion, a Flemish Bond brick farm built in 1817, stands as Fairfield’s oldest surviving landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.travelbutlercounty.com/explore/fairfield/history",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Judge John Cleves Symmes bought 330,000 acres in 1787, calling the area the “garden spot,” which became the foundation for Fairfield.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.fairfield-city.org/533/History-of-Fairfield",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pre‑European Hopewell and Adena peoples once built earthworks around Fairfield, many of which were later cleared for farmland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairfield%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fairfield’s former hamlets—Symmes Corner, Fair Play, Furmandale, and Stockton—merged into the city when it incorporated in 1955.",
